most of us know the Banshee as the  Whelan harbouring of death but where  does the legend originate from in Celtic  and Irish folklore the Banshee is a  fairy who scream is believed to be an  omen of death the Banshee screams served  as a warning that the imminent death of  a family member approaches it is  believed that each family had a banshee  of their own the banshees a spirit that  can appear in a host of different forms  the spirit is always female  but her age clothing and color of hair  can differ drastically the origins of  the Banshee can be traced all the way  back to the eighth century with the  first tales of women singing songs of  sorrow to mourn someone's death these  women were known as Keener's and often  accepted alcohol as payment this meant  that they were seen as sinners and were  often doomed to become banshees once  they had died banshees are often seen as  hateful and angry spirits however there  are some banshees who had a strong  connection with their family in life and  continued to watch over them in death  when these banshees manifests themselves  they often appear as beautiful and  enchanting women that sing songs of  concern and love for their families  these songs can be heard several days  before the death of a family member and  are often heard by the person whom the  songs concern the more common depiction  of a banshee is an angry spirit who in  life hated their families and this hate  is further amplified by death  these banshees do not sing a soothing  song their screams and howls are  chilling to the bone they do not appear  to warn a family member but rather to  celebrate the death of somebody they  despised in other Irish mythology  banshees are the ghost of a young girl  who suffered a brutal death it is said  that these banshees appear as an old  woman with rotten teeth and long  fingernails she wears rags and her eyes  are blood-red and filled with hate  looking directly into her eyes who cause  immediate death these banshees derive  pleasure from taking life and have been  known to actively seek out victims  banshees also appear in Scottish  folklore and there are some key  differences between those in the Irish  legends they are referred to as the bee  and knee otherwise known as the  washerwoman and they are also a symbol  of approaching death Libyan knee is not  doomed to be a banshee forever they will  only remain one until they reach the age  that they would have died in life as  they are not sentenced to mourn the  death of others forever it implies that  they are not born a fairy like the other  banshees in Irish mythology the B&amp;E does  not weigh all like other banshees upon  the death of a person she would be seen  washing blood from funeral shrouds in  the nearby river it is believed that the  B&amp;E was able to interact with humans  more than any other banshee if they  spoke with a human they would have to  answer three of her questions and in  return they would be able to ask three  of their own and be granted a wish for  anything they desired it is important to  note that the vast amount of banshees do  not bring death they are messengers  given families time to prepare for the  inevitable  if you've enjoyed this video then please  go ahead and leave it a like consider  subscribing if folklore mythology is  something that interests you  if you have anything you'd like to  discuss or have any ideas for future  videos then please go ahead and let me  know in the comments below as always  I've been your host mythology and  folklore explained
 

